            <titlepage>
                <titlepage-items/>
            </titlepage>
            <abstract>
                <p>
                    We show that <m>n^2 \equiv n \pmod{2}</m> for every integer <m>n</m>, and we cite a work of every kind we could think of along the way.
                    The result is classical; the citations are the point.
                </p>
            </abstract>
        </frontmatter>

        <section xml:id="section-result">
            <title>The result</title>

            <p>
                That a square has the parity of its root is in every textbook <xref ref="biblio-author-first-course"/>, and several surveys repeat it <xref ref="biblio-writer-reader-parity biblio-marge-squares biblio-reader-thesis"/>.
                The proof we give follows <xref ref="biblio-author-first-course" detail="Theorem 2.3"/>.
            </p>

            <theorem xml:id="theorem-square-parity">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For every integer <m>n</m>,
                        <md>
                            n^2 \equiv n \pmod{2}.
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <proof>
                    <p>
                        The product <m>n(n - 1) = n^2 - n</m> has two consecutive factors, one of which is even.
                    </p>
                </proof>
            </theorem>

            <p>
                A machine-checked version of <xref ref="theorem-square-parity"/> was announced at a meeting <xref ref="biblio-writer-formal"/>, a longer account is a preprint <xref ref="biblio-marge-preprint"/>, and the folklore around it is collected in a handbook chapter <xref ref="biblio-reader-folklore"/>.
            </p>
        </section>

